  "notes": "West leads the ♠J against 6NT. South has 9 tricks in top cards (3 spades, 3 hearts, and 3 clubs). South will be able to develop enough extra tricks in diamonds unless East has length in that suit. If diamonds only provide 2 extra tricks, South can fall back on clubs.\n\nAfter winning the spade opening lead, South should start by leading a low diamond towards the South hand. East cannot afford to play the ♦A and South wins the ♦Q. South crosses back to dummy and leads another low diamond. Again East must duck and South wins the ♦J. West's discard means that South must shift his attack to the club suit.\n\nIt would be too dangerous for South to cash the top club honours. East might have started with 4 clubs to the ♣J. After winning the ♣J, East could cash the ♦A.\n\nSouth can be completely sure of his contract by crossing to the dummy and leading a club to the ♣9 or ♣10. South does not care if West can win the ♣J. West has no diamonds left to return and South has 12 tricks.",
  "commentary_paragraphs": [
    "West leads the ♠J against 6NT. South has 9 tricks in top cards (3 spades, 3 hearts, and 3 clubs). South will be able to develop enough extra tricks in diamonds unless East has length in that suit. If diamonds only provide 2 extra tricks, South can fall back on clubs.",
    "After winning the spade opening lead, South should start by leading a low diamond towards the South hand. East cannot afford to play the ♦A and South wins the ♦Q. South crosses back to dummy and leads another low diamond. Again East must duck and South wins the ♦J. West's discard means that South must shift his attack to the club suit.",
